# CMS inventory sync

Live website + admin CMS read **Cloudflare D1**. `app/cms/seed-products.ts` is the repo mirror. The two are kept in step automatically while `npm run dev` runs (see "CMS sync" in the README); when it does not, the sync commands do it.

## Is the dev server running?

Check with `lsof -nP -iTCP:5173 -sTCP:LISTEN`.

- **Running:** make the change in the **local admin** (`http://localhost:5173/admin`, or log in with `POST /dashboard/session` using `DASHBOARD_USERNAME` / `DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` from `.dev.vars` and `PUT /api/admin/products/{id}`), or edit `app/cms/seed-products.ts` directly. Either way the dev server pushes it to production within seconds and leaves the seed file as the diff to commit. Do not run a sync command as well. `GET /api/admin/cms-sync` says whether the local database is in step with production (`settledAt`, `error`); the admin shows the same above every screen when the sync fails. If the local database looks stale, that is a bug in the sync to fix — never a reason to hand-run a pull while the server is up.
- **Not running:** edit `app/cms/seed-products.ts`, then `npx vite-node scripts/cms-sync.mts --remote --products` (needs `npx wrangler login`). The local database catches up the next time the dev server starts. If the production admin may have been used since the last commit, pull first (`npx vite-node scripts/cms-sync.mts --remote --pull --products`) so the push does not overwrite that edit.

## Adding a new product

Every new card records where it was bought: `acquiredFrom`, the platform and the seller (`Marktplaats – <seller>`, `Vinted – <username>`, `Cardmarket – <username>`, or the person's name for a direct buy), next to `acquiredAt` and `cost`. The business's purchase records need it. If Timo did not say, ask before adding the card.

Photos: use the ones Timo sends. If he sent none, look at the PSA cert number:

- **Below 78,000,000:** psacard.com has no scans of the slab. Until around that cert number, PSA only imaged the higher service tiers. Do not ask: Timo photographs the card when it arrives. Add it as `concept` without `images`, and say the photos are still due.
- **78,000,000 and up:** ask Timo where the photos are.

With photos in hand:

1. Put the slab photos in `seed/media/` as `<cert>_front.jpg` and `<cert>_back.jpg`, then `npm run cms:seed-media`.
2. Add a row for each photo to `seed/cms-media.json` with `"folder": "Slabs"` — every product image belongs in the **Slabs** folder of the media library, never at the top level (`npm test` checks this).
3. Add the product to `app/cms/seed-products.ts` (`images` are `/media/<cert>_front.jpg`, `/media/<cert>_back.jpg`) with its story in `about` (see **Card stories**), then sync as described above.

## Card stories

Every card's page carries a longer story under its banner, in `about` (admin: **About this card**), shown as **About this {title}**. It is what makes one card's page different from the next, so search engines index them, and it stays when the card sells. `npm test` checks every card has one.

- Format: plain text with inline markdown (`**bold**`, `[text](url)`), split into sections by `## Heading` lines. Two or three sections, about 150–250 words in all: **The card** (the print, its number and rarity, who drew it, what the art shows), a section on the set or the card's story, and **The grade** (what the grade means, subgrades for BGS, a link to the PSA cert: `https://www.psacard.com/cert/<cert>`).
- Facts only, each one checked: set, release date, rarity at that number, illustrator. Bulbapedia's card and set pages first (through `web.archive.org/web/2025/<url>` when Cloudflare stops the fetch), pkmncards.com or Serebii next. Leave out what no source confirms; never describe artwork from memory. When the number turns out to be a different print than the `description` says (a regular holo called a Full Art), fix the `description` too and tell Timo, because his Marktplaats and Vinted ads may say the same.
- Never a price, a sale, or condition beyond what the slab says. English, written for collectors, no sales talk.

## Two paths — always finish both sides of the mirror

### A. User asks in chat to change inventory (price, sold, reserved, concept, copy, Marktplaats/Vinted URL, …)

Do **all** of these before saying done:

1. Change the product as described above, and update tests that assert those fields.
2. If the card has `marktplaatsUrl` and price/listing status changed → update, mark reserved, or delete Marktplaats per `marktplaats-listings` rules (Vraagprijs = shop − €0,01).
3. If the card has `vintedUrl` and price/listing status changed → update, mark reserved, or delete the Vinted listing per `vinted-listings` rules (same price as the current Marktplaats Vraagprijs).

Status flags, one at a time: `concept` (not listed yet), none (live), `reserved` (sold, on its way, money not in — stays in the shop without a price, saying **This card is reserved**; ads stay up marked reserved; out of relist and price suggestions), `sold` (money in — off the shop, ads deleted; its page stays up and in the sitemap, marked sold, without the sale price). “Gereserveerd”, “sold but still shipping”, “waiting for the payout” all mean `reserved`, never `sold`. Every reserved or sold card records when and where it sold: `soldAt` (date), `soldTime` (`HH:mm`, Amsterdam time) and `soldVia` (`vinted` or `marktplaats`), so the sales can be read by weekday, hour and marketplace. Sales before 29 September 2026 have no `soldTime`. A price may carry cents (`'€40,50'`, as the sale came in); the books keep them and the website shows whole euros — X,99 as the euro above it (`€44,99` → `€45`), any other cents rounded down (`€40,50` → `€40`).

**Sold cards keep one photo, on their own.** Once a sale has settled, the dev server (`vite/sold-photos.ts`, part of the auto-sync) cuts the card down to a 400 px WebP of the slab front — `images: ['/media/<stem>-front-sold.webp']`, media folder **Sold** — and removes the back photo, both full-size originals (bucket, and `seed/media` for a committed one, regenerating `app/cms/seed-media.ts`) and the branded ad in `public/ads`, then publishes that. Every other field stays: the record is the tax report's source. So when marking a card sold, set the flags and leave `images` alone; do not remove or shrink photos by hand, and do not put the full photos back. When no dev server was running, the next `npm run dev` does it. The admin lists sold cards on the Products screen's **Sold** tab.

### B. User changed something in the admin CMS

Admin saves already update **live D1 / website**, and a running dev server writes them into the seed file on its own. If no dev server was running, pull (`npx vite-node scripts/cms-sync.mts --remote --pull --products`) before touching the seed file.

1. Update tests if assertions no longer match.
2. If they changed **price** (or took a listing down) on a card with `marktplaatsUrl` or `vintedUrl` → still update Marktplaats/Vinted the same way as path A. CMS does not touch either marketplace.

## Commands

| Command | Direction |
| --- | --- |
| `npx vite-node scripts/cms-sync.mts --remote --products` | seed → production D1 |
| `npx vite-node scripts/cms-sync.mts --remote --pull --products` | production D1 → seed |
| `npm run cms:push` / `npm run cms:pull` | same for the local database, all parts |
| `npm run cms:push:remote` / `npm run cms:pull:remote` | production, all parts (a full push also syncs media in R2) |

## Do not

- Add a card without `acquiredFrom`.
- Stop after editing `seed-products.ts` alone when no dev server is running.
- Set `sold: true` for a card whose money has not arrived; that card is `reserved: true` until it has.
- Edit a sold card's `images`, delete its photos from the media library, or re-add its full photos: the dev server keeps exactly the one small front photo, and puts that back.
- Run seed→D1 sync when seed is behind CMS without pulling first (that overwrites live CMS edits).
- Assume deploy alone refreshed product rows (only empty DB seed + one-shot `cmsSeedVersion` migrations do that).
